9. SESSION
10. LONG_TERM
11. ARCHIVED

Use the exact naming convention already established by the project.

Do not add additional memory types.

============================================================
3. MEMORY RECORD
============================================================

Create a strongly typed MemoryRecord contract.

It must support the architecture-defined concepts:

- id
- memory type
- owner
- scope
- content
- metadata
- priority
- security level
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- expiresAt / TTL where applicable
- retention information
- version
- source/reference information where required
- lifecycle state

Do NOT blindly invent fields.

Cross-reference the architecture document and implement the
required fields defined there.

The record must be:

- type-safe
- serializable
- immutable from the consumer perspective where practical
- versionable

Avoid any.

============================================================
4. MEMORY CONTENT
============================================================

Memory content must support structured data.

Do not force everything into a string.

Support a safe JSON-compatible structure.

Define a recursive JSON value type if the project does not
already have one.

Do not use:

any

unknown values should be validated before entering the memory system.

============================================================
5. MEMORY LIFECYCLE
============================================================

Implement lifecycle states according to the architecture.

At minimum inspect and support the required concepts for:

created
active
expired
archived
deleted

Use the exact architecture terminology if different.

Implement lifecycle transition contracts.

Invalid transitions must produce a typed error.

Example:

ACTIVE → ARCHIVED

may be valid.

DELETED → ACTIVE

must not become silently valid.

Do not invent transition rules without checking the specification.

============================================================
6. MEMORY OWNERSHIP
============================================================

Implement ownership concepts defined by the architecture.

Support the distinction between:

- user
- project
- workspace
- organization
- agent/system ownership

Use typed identifiers.

Do not use arbitrary strings everywhere if existing scalar types
can represent them safely.

============================================================
7. ACCESS CONTROL
============================================================

Implement the foundation for memory access control.

The architecture defines access across agent groups and memory types.

Create a typed access model supporting:

READ
WRITE
UPDATE
DELETE

Use a clear permission representation.

Do not implement authentication.

Do not implement a real identity provider.

Do not connect to user accounts.

This sprint only implements the authorization contract.

============================================================
8. SECURITY LEVEL
============================================================

Implement the architecture-defined security classification.

Inspect the specification for the canonical values.

Do not invent additional levels.

Security level must be part of the memory record or its metadata
where specified by the architecture.

============================================================
